[EncDescription_data/units/car/ger/horch_830_b1]
The Horch 830 B1 car was originally intended for commercial use, but was re-equipped for army use in 1937. Most of the manufactured vehicles had canvas covers and cabriolet bodies without doors. The car was produced from 1935 to 1938. The commercial modification differed from the military command car by an all-metal varnished body, chrome-plated bumpers, and doors with crank-operated windows. There were also some modifications in the design of the windshield and windshield wipers. 

[EncDescription_data/units/car/ger/BMW_R75_w_MG_34]
BMW R75 motorcycle with sidecar

Experiments in using motorcycles for military purposes began in Germany in the 1920s. The restrictions arising out of the Treaty of Versailles had made motorized infantry illegal, but did not mention motorcycles. Motorcycles were also much cheaper than other vehicles, which was an important consideration for the cash-strapped state. The concept of using highly mobile, highly versatile motorcycle units as part of offensive operations was complete by the 1930s.

A large number of motorcycles was used by the Wehrmacht, including both specially made military vehicles, and captured or requisitioned civilian models. The large variety of models made maintenance and repair difficult. To address the problem, a requirement was issued for an off-road capable army motorcycle. Based on the results of the competition, a BMW prototype was accepted for service. Serial production of the new motorcycle, designated R75, was launched in May of 1941.

R75 motorcycles, both unarmed and with mounted machine-guns, were heavily used throughout the war. In 1941-42, Wehrmacht units had full-fledged motorcycle battalions and companies. German motorcycle units, suddenly attacking important enemy positions from the rear, became one of the symbols of Blitzkrieg, alongside panzers and Stukas. By 1943, as the Wehrmacht operations were becoming increasingly more defensive in nature, most motorcycle units were disbanded. However, motorcycles remained in service, especially with panzer and mechanized divisions, mostly used for recon and courier duties. The R75 was well liked by the troops for its ruggedness and reliability, with many examples remaining in active use for many years after the war. A total of 16,545 motorcycles of this type were manufactured before production ended in October of 1944.

[EncDescription_data/units/car/ger/Kubelwagen_afrika]
Light Utility Roadster, Kübelwagen VW Typ 82

Requirements for a new utility roadster were published in Germany as early as 1929. The vehicle was to have a 4x2 suspension, large wheels, strengthened suspension springs, and an open-top, with a tent available for bad weather. In 1933 a vehicle matching the requirements went into production, but proved less than successful, being phased out in 1940. Shortage of utility vehicles in the military led to wide-scale adoption of civilian vehicles, often ill-fitted for military needs. A different solution was needed.

In early 1938, Ferdinand Porsche the famous designer was asked to produce a light military vehicle. By November of the same year a prototype was ready for testing; however, it needed some work, and finally went into mass production in spring of 1940. Several modifications were produced, including a machine-gun-armed reconnaissance vehicle, an amphibious vehicle, moving repair labs, and others. Due to its rugged construction, off-road capabilities, and reliability the Kübelwagen became very popular with the troops. The vehicle was used to transport personnel (including recon units), as a staff and communications car, and to tow light artillery pieces.

The Kübelwagen remained in service throughout the war, seeing action on all fronts. By the time the production ended in 1945, Volkswagen had produced about 52,000 vehicles of this type.


[EncDescription_data/units/car/ger/Kubelwagen]
Light Utility Roadster, Kübelwagen VW Typ 82

Requirements for a new utility roadster were published in Germany as early as 1929. The vehicle was to have a 4x2 suspension, large wheels, strengthened suspension springs, and an open-top, with a tent available for bad weather. In 1933 a vehicle matching the requirements went into production, but proved less than successful, being phased out in 1940. Shortage of utility vehicles in the military led to wide-scale adoption of civilian vehicles, often ill-fitted for military needs. A different solution was needed.

In early 1938, Ferdinand Porsche the famous designer was asked to produce a light military vehicle. By November of the same year a prototype was ready for testing; however, it needed some work, and finally went into mass production in spring of 1940. Several modifications were produced, including a machine-gun-armed reconnaissance vehicle, an amphibious vehicle, moving repair labs, and others. Due to its rugged construction, off-road capabilities, and reliability the Kübelwagen became very popular with the troops. The vehicle was used to transport personnel (including recon units), as a staff and communications car, and to tow light artillery pieces.

The Kübelwagen remained in service throughout the war, seeing action on all fronts. By the time the production ended in 1945, Volkswagen had produced about 52,000 vehicles of this type.


[EncDescription_data/units/car/ger/Opel_Blitz_Tent]
Opel Blitz S Light Truck

The history of one of the most famous trucks of the war, the Opel Blitz, began in 1930. The first variants of the vehicle included a 1.5 and a 2-ton truck; a 2.5-ton variant was introduced in 1931. Inexpensive and reliable, the trucks proved popular. A new vehicle taxation system was introduced in Germany in 1935, intended to encourage development of off-road capable vehicles, which would be of more use to the Wehrmacht in case of war. In 1936, this led to a 3-ton Opel Blitz S, which fit the new requirement. Subsequently, it was this very vehicle that became the most mass-produced vehicle of the Wehrmacht. 

Without a doubt, the Blitz was a successful design, widely admired by the troops, despite being a civilian vehicle modified for the military. Even though it was a two-wheel drive vehicle, the Blitz was quite capable of navigating rough terrain. The truck was also very durable, and easily repaired in the field, proving reliable even under rough conditions of the Eastern Front. The fact that the vehicle used gasoline was also an advantage, since diesel fuel was always in short supply in Germany. 

Numerous modifications of the Opel Blitz were produced over the years. A 1.5-ton variant was produced between 1938 and 1942. A half-track variant was introduced in October of 1942, and an all-wheel drive variant was in production from 1940 to 1944. The stock chassis became a base for fuel trucks, communication vehicles, ambulances, staff cars, mobile radio-locators, and other utility vehicles. Several make-shift modifications in the field even converted the Blitz into a self-propelled flak, with a small-caliber gun installed in the back, and the cabin receiving additional armour plating. 

The Blitz remained in production until the end of the war. When Allied bombers destroyed the Opel factory in August of 1944, licensed production was quickly set up at Daimler Benz. Civilian variants of the truck remained in production long after the war, until 1954.


[EncDescription_data/units/car/ger/SdKfz_7]
Zugkraftwagen 8t Sd Kfz 7, 8-ton half-track prime mover

The Sd Kfz medium prime mover was developed in 1934 by Krauss-Maffei. As other German prime movers, the vehicle was a half-track, which enabled it to navigate across rough terrain. The rear section of the vehicle contained seating for the gun crew, which could be covered with a tent in case of bad weather.

The most mass-produced of German prime movers of the war, the Sd Kfz 7 was heavily used on all fronts throughout the war, proving capable and reliable. The vehicle was used to tow the famous 88mm Flak guns, 152mm howitzers, 105mm guns, and even light armoured vehicles. Several modifications of the vehicle were produced, including those with an armoured interior; a slightly different variant was also built in Italy. Altogether, 12,187 of these vehicles were produced.

The prime mover was also used as a basis for the 20mm Sd Kfz 7/1 and 37mm Sd Kfz 7/2 self-propelled flak.

[EncDescription_data/units/car/rus/GAZ_67]
GAZ-67 Off-road Car M1943

In January of 1941, V. A. Malyshev, the People’s Comissar for Mechanical Engineering, entrusted the GAZ design bureau with the development of a light, all-wheel drive, off-road army vehicle. By March 25, the GAZ-64 prototype had been built, which was based on the American Bantam. It entered tests in April, simultaneously with the competing AR-NATI prototype. In July of 1941, the GAZ-64 entered mass production, as it had been found to be the more manufacturable of the two vehicles.
However, field use of the GAZ-64 exposed a number of drawbacks, especially the narrow wheelbase which affected riding stability and which made it difficult to drive in a column of wider vehicles. Improvements began in February of 1943, and the new prototypes entered field tests that summer. On September 23, 1943, the new vehicle, designated the GAZ-67, replaced its predecessor on the assembly line. The new car had been given a wider wheelbase of 1446mm, a new carburetor, which increased engine power, and featured numerous other minor improvements.
The mass production of the GAZ-67 lasted until the end of 1944, during which span a total of 3137 cars were manufactured. The GAZ-67 was eventually replaced by the GAZ-67B, and the base of the GAZ-67 chassis also came to serve as the base of the BA-64B armored car.
The GAZ-67 was primarily used as a command vehicle, courier car, and light anti-tank artillery tractor. It was durable, easy to maintain, undemanding in terms of fuel quality, and boasted good off-road capability.
Those GAZ-67 vehicles that survived the war continued to serve in the Soviet Army until their gradual withdrawal in the 1950s due to tear and wear.

[EncDescription_data/units/car/rus/ZIS_151]
ZIS-151 All-Wheel Drive Truck M1948

During the Great Patriotic War, the United States shipped numerous six-wheel trucks to the USSR, some of which were all-wheel drive, as a part of the lend-lease program. The US vehicles were superior in off-road capability to the Russian low-power GAZ-ААА and ZIS-6, and they established an excellent reputation amongst the Russian military. Their obvious advantages spurred the Soviets in their own development of such vehicles.
The new six-wheeler was launched at the beginning of 1945. It was based in part on the Studebaker US6, and on some domestic models. Two prototypes – with single and double-tire wheels – were completed in 1946 and entered field tests in 1947. In these tests, they competed with each other and with other lend-lease trucks. The tests showed better off-road capability and lower fuel consumption of the single-tire vehicle. However, military command preferred the variant that was more closely similar to the Studebaker US6. Mass production of the new truck, designated the ZIS-151, was launched in the spring of 1948, and the line remained in active production until 1958, when ZIS-151 was finally replaced by its heavily upgraded variant, the ZIL-157.
In terms of design, the ZIS-151 was a six-wheel, gas-engine, all-wheel drive truck. It was widely used as a cargo and personnel carrier and artillery tractor, and became the Soviet Army’s primary truck of the 1950s. The vehicle saw frequent improvements over the course of its service. The ZIS-151A, for instance, featured a winch and an alternate cab. The ZIS-151 chassis became the foundation of numerous special-purpose vehicles, including the BM-13 multiple rocket launcher and the BTR-152 APC.

[EncDescription_data/units/car/rus/gaz_aaa]
Mass production of the GAZ-AAA three-axle truck began at the Gorky Automobile Plant in November 1934. the design of the truck was derived from that of the GAZ-AA vehicle. It was upgraded, i.e. the rear drive axle was replaced with a two-axle trolley. The increase in the number of wheels boosted the automobile’s cross-country capability on tracks and forest roads. The cross-country capability on snow and soft ground could be further improved by adding Overall chains to the rear wheels. 
The GAZ-AA was mostly intended for transporting troops and cargo. Moreover, the GAZ-AAA chassis was used to manufacture BA-6 medium armored vehicles, GAZ-05-193 staff buses, fuel trucks and radar stations. During the war, the three-axle vehicles also carried SU-12 artillery systems, HMG Maxim or HMGs DShK. As many as 37,373 vehicles of this type were made from 1934 to 1943. 

[EncDescription_data/units/car/rus/gaz_m1]
The Gorky Automobile Plant (GAZ) started manufacturing the GAZ-M1 (“Emka”) car in 1936. The car was derived from the Ford-V8-40 vehicle, designed in 1933. However, the prototype was modified by the Gorky Plant’s design department, taking into account lack of proper roads in Russia. The car frame was reinforced, the number of springs was increased from two to four, spoke wheels were replaced with pressed solid wheels, and their diameter was increased. The engine was modified and boosted from 40 to 50 hp. 
Thanks to these improvements, the car became stronger and much more enduring.
The GAZ-M1 vehicle had relatively good speed specifications and could run as fast as 105 km/h on the highway. Average fuel consumption amounted to 14.5 l per 100 km. The GAZ-M1 was designed as a commercial vehicle, but its cross-country capability on unpaved roads was advanced enough to derive the BA-20 armored vehicle from it. 
The GAZ-M1 was in production until 1943. A total of 62,888 vehicles of this type were manufactured, most of them staying in service with the Red Army until the end of the war. Quite a lot of models were derived from the GAZ-M1. 

[EncDescription_data/units/car/rus/nkl_16_mod_1941]
The first variant of the three-ski NKL-16 snowmobile was made in 1937 under the supervision of chief designer Nikolai Andreyev. The snowmobile was mass-produced as transport and medical models. An updated model was manufactured in 1940-1941. It had four skis and was designated NKL-16/41.
The snowmobiles of all manufactured types, including NKL-16, NKL-16/41 and NKL-16/42, were widely employed at fronts of the Great Patriotic War, especially in the winter of 1941-1942. They were used for quick communication, delivery of military cargo, reconnaissance, assault and combat missions. When delivering assault groups, the snowmobiles not only carried servicemen with full armament, but also towed 18 to 20 skiers, using special cables. In combat conditions, they towed sledges at the sides, carrying soldiers armed with a MG and a soldier with ammo. Moreover, machine-gun fire could be delivered by soldiers sitting in the snowmobile through hatches located at the top.
Snowmobile transport battalions successfully accomplished numerous intelligence and communication missions of the Soviet Army command and worked with combat snowmobile units, using NKL-26 vehicles. This made it possible not only to make unexpected raids aimed at destroying enemy infantry, but also to quickly deliver assault groups.

[EncDescription_data/units/car/rus/voroshilovetz]
The introduction of powerful artillery systems in the Red Army in the mid-1930s, as well as the emergence of new medium and heavy tanks weighing up to 28 tons, made it necessary to design a powerful tractor. The Kharkov Locomotive-Building Plant received the order to design and manufacture such a tractor in 1935. 
Two tractors were already produced in 1936 and underwent factory and field tests in the next two years. In March 1937 one of them was demonstrated to the Soviet government and received highest approval. Mass production of the “Voroshilovets” began in 1939. About 800 vehicles were made before the beginning of the Great Patriotic War, 228 of them were handed over to the artillery. Another 170 were manufactured during wartime. 
During the war “Voroshilovets” tractors were used quite effectively on all fronts, for transportation of heavy cargo, but primarily heavy artillery.

[EncDescription_data/units/car/rus/Gaz_mm_Kursk1943]
GAZ-MM Truck, 1942

The story of the famous 'one-and-a-half' truck began in 1929 when the Soviet government signed a contract with the Ford Company. The American company was to assist in building a large automobile plant in Gorky (which was called Nizhny Novgorod back then) and to sell a production license for Ford AA trucks. The first trucks, built from the assembly sets provided by Ford, were manufactured as early as 1930. The assembly line of the new plant began working on April 1, 1932; by the end of the year, sixty new GAZ-AA trucks rolled out of it every day. Some time later, the plants in Moscow and Rostov-on-Don joined in the production of these trucks. In 1933, the GAZ-AA was upgraded. Among other minor changes in design, a more powerful GAZ M-1 engine was installed on it. The upgraded car was designated GAZ-MM. Since the beginning of the war its design was drastically simplified – for example, in 1941-1942 the cab of the 'one-and-a-half' was tented and had no doors, and all metal parts were replaced with wooden where possible. In 1943 the production of a new modification with a wooden cab began.

Many of these trucks were used in field for transporting people and cargo and towing light guns, and they were used as a mount for anti-aircraft machine guns. At the same time it should be stated that a commercial vehicle such as GAZ-MM was in fact ill suited for the army – it did not benefit from all-wheel drive, so its handling abilities left much to be desired. Among its advantages there were high reliability, low maintenance, and resilience even in difficult circumstances, particularly for example where fuel quality was an issue.

A total of 102,300 GAZ-MM trucks were produced at GAZ alone between 1941 and 1945. The serial production of the car continued until 1951.

[EncDescription_data/units/car/rus/ZIS_5]
ZIS-5 Truck, 1941

The ZIS-5 truck traces its roots to the American Autocar-SA truck, which had been produced in the USSR since 1931 as the AMO-2 (and later the AMO-3). When the ZIS-5 went into production in 1933, it differed from the American prototype through a full-scale design simplification and adaptation for mass production given the conditions of Soviet industry. Almost all nonferrous metals were excluded from its design; only steel, cast-iron, and wood remained.

After the famous 'one-and-a-half' (GAZ-MM), the ZIS-5 was the most widely used RKKA truck, though both were designed as civilian and not combat vehicles. In the field, the 'three-ton' was used very widely for the transport of cargo and people, for towing anti-tank guns, and as mounts for anti-aircraft machine guns. The truck proved its worth as a very reliable, low maintenance and easy-to-repair vehicle, the main drawback of which was insufficient passing ability. The vehicle received several modifications, the most notable of which was ZIS-5V – a simplified wartime variant which entered production in 1942.

The vehicle was produced at the ZIS factory of Moscow until 1948 (with a short break in 1942 caused by the evacuation of the factory). Beginning in 1942 it was produced in Ulyanovsk, and in 1944 a second production line began in Miass. At the latter factory, the ZIS-5 was produced until 1958, and its modifications remained in production until 1965.

[EncDescription_data/units/car/rus/M_72]
Production of M-72 motorcycles begun in 1941 and continued to 1960, while it's modifications were produced until early 80's.
Developed in pre-war year 1940, M-72 was a full copy of German BMW-R71. Each M-72 was equipped with DP light machine gun, so it was designated as combat vehicle by GABTU RKKA (main artillery and tank department of Red Army). LMG was attached to special rotatable platform, enabling firing it from still motorcycle or even on the move. Tubular blackets at front and rear of the sidecar made firing at front or rear directions possible. Very small quantities of motorcycles equipped with 82mm mortars instead of machine guns and with wodden or plywood sidecars were issued to armed forces too. Usually bags containing spare parts and ammo were attached to motorcycle.

[EncDescription_data/units/car/usa/Dodge_WC_51]
Dodge WC-51 All-Wheel Drive 3/4 ton Truck

The US Army used military pickup trucks as far back as World War I, but the vehicles lacked all-wheel drive. After WWI, the army stopped placing orders for such vehicles, as Congress became increasingly reluctant to spend on warfare. The number of active tank units also dwindled. At the same time, all-wheel drive jeeps and light trucks became quite popular in the commercial segment, and were produced by a number of companies, Dodge in particular.

The situation changed in 1939, when Dodge received an orders to produce a new, light, all-wheel drive military vehicle. The first model, designated the VC-1, was based on a commercial vehicle and resembled a civilian truck. It was continuously modernized and improved while the design was simplified. However, two main problems – the narrow wheelbase and the use of standard-issue civilian tires – were not yet resolved.
In 1941, Dodge won the military contract for production of the 3/4 ton truck. Compared with its predecessors, this new vehicle was significantly improved, featuring a much higher 750kg payload, wide wheelbase, and military-issue wide tires. It entered mass production in 1942.

The Dodge WC-51 was an all-wheel drive, open-top, canvas-cover 3/4 ton truck, capable of carrying an infantry squad or a light gun crew. Many variants were produced from the basic model, including a command vehicle, ambulances, and other such specialized units. Even a six-wheel variant was being built by the end of the war. The vehicle was quite a success, combining durability, off-road capability, and reliability. For this reason, it was acknowledged as the best-in-class wartime vehicle.

Dodge WC-51 trucks were used on all fronts where the US Army fought. Many of these trucks were lend-leased, particularly to Great Britain and the USSR. The latter used these trucks as tractors for their 57mm ZIS-2 and 76mm ZIS-3 guns, especially in tank-destroyer artillery regiments. Because of the non-standard payload, the Russians semi-officially designated the truck the Dodge 3/4. Over 253 thousand 3/4 ton Dodge trucks were produced over the course of the war, many of which were still being used by the US Army into the 1960s, the Korean War included.

[EncDescription_data/units/car/usa/GMC_CCKW_353]
GMC CCKW 353 All-Wheel Drive Truck 

In 1938, General Motors designed and built its first 6x4 Army truck, the T-16 Special. It had a payload of 5 tons on the highway and 2.5 tons off road. A number of these trucks were delivered to the US Army the same year.
At the same time, the company negotiated a contract with France for 2000 trucks. The newly improved GMC T-16 Special, designated the ACKWX 353, featured a widened wheelbase and front-wheel drive. But these new trucks were not ultimately shipped to France, but to Great Britain instead.
The US Army decided to adopt six-wheelers in 1941. As expected, GMC won the contract, based on an improved variant of their ACKWX 353, which entered mass production the same year.

The GMC CCKW 353 was a powerful military-grade 6x6 truck, which like its predecessor offered an off-road payload of 2.5 tons and highway payload of 5 tons. It was designed to carry a squad of soldiers and their weapons. US six-wheelers (from GMC and other manufacturers) were rightfully considered the best trucks of the World War II. Their combination of payload, off-road capability and reliability was unsurpassed. A number of variants were made, including the CCKW 352 which offered a different wheelbase, and the CCW 353 which lacked front-wheel drive. Other US trucks series offered yet more variation, particularly in the cab department. The GMC CCKW 353 served as the basis for several special-purpose trucks, including tank trucks, rescue vehicles and so on.

The GMC CCKW 353 was categorized as the US Army's principle six-wheeler truck. Trucks made by other companies were mostly lend-leased, to avoid compatibility issues. The latter were used wherever the US Army fought, from North Africa to Italy and France to the Pacific. On these varied fronts, the CCKW 353 was used as a personnel and cargo carrier and a divisional artillery tractor. Many more of them were lend-leased to Great Britain, Australia, New Zealand and the USSR. The latter also used these trucks as the base for the renowned Katyusha multiple rocket launchers. During the postwar years, these trucks had a long service history with the US Army, and played a vital role in the Korean War.

[EncDescription_data/units/car/usa/Studebacker_US6]
Studebaker US6 6x6 Truck

It became evident in 1941 that General Motors could not keep pace with the huge demand of the US Army for three-axle trucks. Part of the order was delegated to the Studebaker Company, which had already begun producing these trucks by the end of 1941. Their main difference was the engine, which had a different valve arrangement and lower compression rate. It was soon determined that the allies could benefit from these irregular trucks – all the more because their engines were better suited for the low quality of Soviet gasoline.

A huge amount of these trucks was supplied to the USSR, where they established a good reputation and wide use. In the Red Army, Studebakers transported people and cargo (notably, instead of the standard 2.5 ton capacity they were capable of taking on 3.5 to 4 tons), and towed 122mm howitzers and 85mm antiaircraft guns. From the spring of 1943, the Studebaker became the standard chassis for the Soviet field rocket launchers – the famous Katyusha. The vehicle was celebrated for its excellent road-reliability, and maneuverability, for there were no three-axle all-wheel-drive trucks produced in USSR at that time. However, their high requirements for fuel quality, compared with Soviet vehicles, was noted as a disadvantage.

The vehicle had several modifications, differing in the length of the wheelbase, the cab type, the hoist, and so on. Besides the all-wheel-drive modification, a 6x4 variant was produced. A total of 187 thousand all-wheel-drive Studebaker US6 and over twenty thousand 6x4 vehicles were produced during the war years.

[EncDescription_data/units/car/usa/Willys_MB]
\t Willys MB US Army Jeep

\t In 1940, US Army issued specifications for a light cross-country and reconnaissance vehicle. The vehicle was to carry four passengers and be as light as possible; initial specifications called for a weight of only 500 lbs. Willys, Bantam and later Ford all entered the competition. It quickly became clear that the weight of 500 lbs was unattainable, so the weight requirement was quickly increased to 1,300 lbs. Field trials commenced in November and December of 1940, where no clear winner was identified, but the Willys design was proven superior. All three designs were ordered into production, but in the end both Bantam and Ford failed to deliver. Bantam was unable to organize large-scale production, and Ford was unable to overcome problems with the reliability of their design. Willys however continued to refine their vehicle. By early 1941, the Willys MA variant was introduced, and the final Willys MB variant became available in August of the same year. It was the MB variant that went into mass production; at its peak, 400 of these jeeps were manufactured daily at the Willys factories alone.
\t Willys MB was without a doubt the most successful light utility vehicle of the era. The Willys jeep’s off-road capabilities, reliability and durability became legendary. In addition to being used by the US Army, Willys jeeps were widely exported to other Allied countries, where they were used in a variety of roles. In Russia, the Willys was even used to tow 45mm artillery pieces, in addition to other roles. A total of 659,000 Willys jeeps were manufactured during the war by Willys and Ford.

[EncDescription_data/units/car/uk/CPM_Ford_FAT_2]
Ford FAT-2 Quad Gun Tractor, 1941

The British Army used specialized all-wheel-drive vehicles to tow artillery pieces, as opposed to the Germans who used half-tracks, or the Russians who used tractors and regular trucks. The concept of such a vehicle was known as FAT, for Field Artillery Tractor.  The first example of the type was developed in 1938 by the Guy Motors company, designated Ant, and went into production the same year.

After the war began in Europe, the British Army need for artillery tractors quickly increased, but this demand could not be fully met by Guy Ant. Morris Commercial also began producing their own vehicle in 1940, designated C8. A separate KT 4 modification was built by Karrier specifically for India. Nevertheless, both of these vehicles still could not meet the needs of the British Army.

A solution was found by ordering additional vehicles in Canada, manufactured by CMP (Canadian Military Pattern) under the Ford and Chevrolet brands (which were otherwise nearly identical). The Canadian design was based on a British project, but was becoming increasingly more different with each new variant. By 1941 the new artillery tractors went into service with the British Army.

The FAT-2 was an all-wheel drive vehicle intended to tow field guns and transport gun crews. The vehicle was rather successful, compact, and reliable, proving capable in adverse weather, particularly in North Africa. The British mainly used the vehicle to transport 18- and 25-pdr guns, and 6- and 17-pdr anti-tank weapons. In addition to the British, the FAT-2 was also used by Australia and Canada. Geographically the vehicle served virtually everywhere in the world, including the Mediterranean, Europe, and South-East Asia. A total of 22,891 CMP tractors of different modifications were produced.

[EncDescription_data/units/car/fr/unic_p107]
Adolf Kegresse, who headed the Russian Emperor’s Garage until 1917, developed a new type of propulsive device for cars and trucks in 1909. He replaced the rear axle of a vehicle with a tracked propulsive device, which considerably boosted its cross-country capability. Kegresse returned to France in 1918 and offered his ideas to a few companies. In the end, the Citroen company got interested in the designer’s work, and this resulted in an entire family of semi-trucked vehicles. The rubber-metal track was a specialty of Kegresse’s propulsive device, though the component was not initially distinguished for advanced reliability. 
The French military got interested in Citroen-Kegresse vehicles, and the P.17 artillery tractor was the first Citroen-Kegresse to enter the French army’s inventory. This was followed by the commissioning of the P.19, developed for cavalry. The P.107 model was designed in 1936, but it was becoming more and more obvious that Citroen could not execute a large-scale order on its own. As a result, the P.107 model was manufactured not only by Citroen, but also by other companies. The vehicle was assembled by the Laffly company under the designation of S15T, but the highest number of semi-track tractors was manufactured by the UNIC company. Over 3,000 UNIC P.107 vehicles of various versions were made before 1940. Alongside with artillery tractors, the UNIC P.107 model for engineer forces and another one for airfields were produced. 
The UNIC P.107 was the main type of French medium artillery tractors. Vehicles of this type were most often used for towing the 75-mm Canon de Campagne Mle.1897 guns, as well as howitzers. Many UNIC P.107 vehicles were used by the Wehrmacht after France was defeated. The Germans derived a high number of specialized hardware units from the tractor, starting with APCs and ending with air defense self-propelled systems.

[EncDescription_data/units/car/pol/polish_fiat_pf_508_lazik]
Poland started manufacturing Polski FIAT-508 and FIAT-518 cars and FIAT-614 and FIAT-621 trucks by Italian licenses in the early 1930s. All these vehicles were used in the Polish army for staff, transport, medical and special purposes. 
In late 1935, engineers Mieczysław Skwierczyński and Tadeusz Tański designed the FIAT-508 Lazik military vehicle (its commercial prototype was called Polski FIAT-508 III Junak). The new automobile in comparison to its predecessor had an open body that ensured quick mounting and dismounting of four men with firearms. The body was made of steel with a basic wooden structure in the rear section. The inner side of the body carried special equipment for military purposes, such as bags for hand grenades and cartridges, and mounts for armaments. Some vehicles carried a 7.92-mm Browning wz.28 machine-gun.
About 1,500 Lazik and about 400 tractors derived from the same Junak were manufactured. The latter could tow a trailer weighing up to 350 kg and were intended for transporting a 37-mm antitank gun. 

[EncDescription_data/units/car/pol/polish_fiat_pf_621_tent]
The Polski FIAT 621L was the most common Polish pre-war truck and the basic military truck in 1939. It was an improved and strengthened copy of the Italian FIAT 621, produced in the PZInz works from 1935 to 1939. The PF-621L was a 2.5t truck with a 46 HP engine. In 1938 the new streamlined cab was introduced. From among 9,500 produced before the war, the Army had 1,400 in 1939, not counting the commissioned civilian vehicles. A small number of heavier PF-621R 3-tonne trucks (on a bus chassis) was also used. PF-621s were used as general service trucks, and also as troop carriers in the 10th Motorized Cavalry Brigade (10 BKZmot) and Warsaw Armored-Motorized Brigade (WBP-M).

[EncDescription_data/units/car/fr/unic_p107]
Adolf Kegresse, quien estuvo al frente del parque de automóviles del zar ruso hasta 1917, desarrolló un nuevo tipo de dispositivo de propulsión para automóviles y camiones en 1909. Reemplazó el eje trasero de un vehículo por un dispositivo de propulsión por oruga, lo que aumentó considerablemente su capacidad todoterreno. Kegresse regresó a Francia en 1918 y ofreció sus ideas a varias empresas. Finalmente, la compañía Citroën se interesó en el trabajo del diseñador, lo que dio como resultado toda una familia de vehículos semioruga. La oruga de goma-metal era una especialidad del dispositivo de propulsión de Kegresse, aunque el componente no se distinguió inicialmente por su alta fiabilidad.
El ejército francés se interesó por los vehículos Citroën-Kegresse, y el tractor de artillería P.17 fue el primer Citroën-Kegresse en entrar en el inventario del ejército francés. A esto le siguió la puesta en servicio del P.19, desarrollado para la caballería. El modelo P.107 se diseñó en 1936, pero cada vez era más evidente que Citroën no podía ejecutar un pedido a gran escala por sí sola. Como resultado, el modelo P.107 fue fabricado no solo por Citroën, sino también por otras empresas. El vehículo fue ensamblado por la compañía Laffly bajo la designación S15T, pero la mayor cantidad de tractores semioruga fue fabricada por la compañía UNIC. Se fabricaron más de 3.000 vehículos UNIC P.107 de varias versiones antes de 1940. Junto a los tractores de artillería, se produjeron el modelo UNIC P.107 para las fuerzas de ingenieros y otro para aeródromos.
El UNIC P.107 fue el principal tipo de tractor de artillería medio francés. Los vehículos de este tipo se utilizaban con mayor frecuencia para remolcar los cañones Canon de Campagne Mle.1897 de 75 mm, así como obuses. Muchos vehículos UNIC P.107 fueron utilizados por la Wehrmacht después de la derrota de Francia. Los alemanes derivaron una gran cantidad de unidades de hardware especializadas del tractor, comenzando con transportes blindados de personal (APC) y terminando con sistemas antiaéreos autopropulsados.

[EncDescription_data/units/car/pol/polish_fiat_pf_508_lazik]
Polonia comenzó a fabricar los automóviles Polski FIAT-508 y FIAT-518 y los camiones FIAT-614 y FIAT-621 bajo licencias italianas a principios de la década de 1930. Todos estos vehículos fueron utilizados en el ejército polaco para fines de estado mayor, transporte, médicos y especiales.
A finales de 1935, los ingenieros Mieczysław Skwierczyński y Tadeusz Tański diseñaron el vehículo militar FIAT-508 Lazik (su prototipo comercial se llamaba Polski FIAT-508 III Junak). El nuevo automóvil, en comparación con su predecesor, tenía una carrocería abierta que aseguraba el montaje y desmontaje rápido de cuatro hombres con armas de fuego. La carrocería era de acero con una estructura básica de madera en la sección trasera. El interior de la carrocería llevaba equipo especial para fines militares, como bolsas para granadas de mano y cartuchos, y soportes para armamento. Algunos vehículos llevaban una ametralladora Browning wz.28 de 7,92 mm.
Se fabricaron alrededor de 1.500 Lazik y unos 400 tractores derivados del mismo Junak. Estos últimos podían remolcar un remolque de hasta 350 kg y estaban destinados al transporte de un cañón antitanque de 37 mm.

[EncDescription_data/units/car/pol/polish_fiat_pf_621_tent]
El Polski FIAT 621L fue el camión polaco de antes de la guerra más común y el camión militar básico en 1939. Era una copia mejorada y reforzada del FIAT 621 italiano, producido en las fábricas de PZInz de 1935 a 1939. El PF-621L era un camión de 2,5 toneladas con un motor de 46 CV. En 1938 se introdujo la nueva cabina aerodinámica. De los 9.500 producidos antes de la guerra, el Ejército tenía 1.400 en 1939, sin contar los vehículos civiles requisados. También se utilizó un pequeño número de camiones más pesados PF-621R de 3 toneladas (sobre un chasis de autobús). Los PF-621 se utilizaron como camiones de servicio general, y también como transportes de tropas en la 10.ª Brigada de Caballería Motorizada (10 BKZmot) y la Brigada Acorazada-Motorizada de Varsovia (WBP-M).